## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a scalable, compliant, and observable MLOps workflow that supports multi-tenant isolation while meeting real-time processing requirements and governance standards.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant architecture with per-tenant resource isolation
- Real-time stream processing with PII redaction at ingestion
- Feature store management for tenant-specific model training
- Canary deployment strategies for safe model rollouts
- Model explainability and drift detection with automated rollback
- Comprehensive data lineage and governance tracking
- Cost management controls at the tenant level
- Late-arrival data handling within specified time windows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: canary deployment decision logic
if (tenantDrift[tenant] < DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
  promoteCanaryToProduction(tenant);
} else {
  rollbackToStableModel(tenant);
  triggerAlert(tenant, 'Model drift detected');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution across tenants?
- What strategies would you use for optimizing costs in a multi-tenant environment?
- How would you ensure data consistency when processing late-arriving events?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect end-to-end edge ML workflows on GCP with enterprise-grade privacy, governance, and operational considerations beyond model accuracy.

## Key Concepts
- Edge model distillation and Vertex AI training pipelines
- Edge artifact distribution via Pub/Sub and Cloud CDN
- Regional canary deployments and per-tenant quotas
- Data residency, CMEK, and DLP for privacy compliance
- On-device telemetry, drift monitoring, and automated rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example config for edge rollout policy
const rollout = {
  region: 'us-east1',
  quota: 1000,
  canary: {
    percentage: 10,
    duration: '2h'
  },
  validation: {
    latencyTarget: '<100ms',
    driftThreshold: 0.05,
    minSampleSize: 1000
  },
  rollback: {
    auto: true,
    cooldown: '30min'
  }
};
```
